# The dev stack's HTTPS front door — deliberately the SAME SHAPE as production.
#
# `heriverse-ansible/role/templates/Caddyfile.j2` terminates TLS on one host and
# reverse-proxies by path to service names on the internal network. This file
# does the same thing on `em.localhost`, so what is exercised here is the
# arrangement that will run on the institutional host: one origin for the app,
# the room, the images and the assets — which is what removes mixed-content and
# makes a token in a query defensible.
#
# The certificate is issued by Caddy's INTERNAL CA (`tls internal`). Trusting it
# is a step for a human with a password — see README-DEV. Until then `curl
# --cacert` works and browsers do not, and that boundary is the honest state of
# a local https: the real answer is a public certificate on a real host.

{
	# a local CA, not ACME: there is nothing public to validate here
	local_certs
	auto_https disable_redirects
}

{$EM_SITE:https://em.localhost} {
	tls internal

	# StratiGraph · EMSTUDIO, on the NODE'S OWN ORIGIN — the editor at
	# `/em/studio/`, the dissemination reader at `/em/read/`.
	#
	# Why it is here at all is the same argument the field assistant made for the
	# camera, one month earlier: a page served on a port of its own cannot do the
	# thing it exists for. Measured in Chrome on 4 September — the Catalog's
	# «open in browser» opened `localhost:5173` and the editor answered «Study
	# unreachable — TypeError: Failed to fetch», from BOTH spellings of the
	# catalogue's address, because the Catalog sends no CORS headers and a
	# different port is a different origin. Same origin, and there is no question
	# to answer.
	#
	# NOT `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` on the Catalog. A permission given to
	# everybody is not the repair of a permission that is missing. If a
	# deployment one day wants EMStudio elsewhere, that origin is NAMED in the
	# configuration.
	#
	# THESE TWO BLOCKS MUST COME BEFORE `/em/*`. Caddy orders `handle` matchers by
	# specificity, so a longer prefix wins — but writing them in the order they
	# are evaluated is what makes the file readable, and the routing table in the
	# end-of measures it rather than trusting it.
	#
	# `handle` and NOT `handle_path`: in development the source is the Vite dev
	# server on the HOST, and its `base` is `/em/studio/` (`frontend/vite.config.ts`).
	# The paths line up exactly, so there is nothing to strip and no rewriting to
	# get wrong — `/em/studio/src/main.ts` is what the browser asks for and what
	# Vite serves.
	handle /em/studio/* {
		# When the dev server is DOWN the answer is a 502 that READS, and it is
		# written once for both routes in `handle_errors` at the bottom of this
		# site — `handle_errors` is not an ordered handler and cannot live inside
		# a `handle` (Caddy says so, and validating is how I found out).
		#
		# What must NOT happen is a fallback answering 200 with some HTML: the
		# one that did cost three days, because a client doing `res.json()`
		# reported «node unreachable» while the node was up. So: no fallback.
		reverse_proxy host.docker.internal:5173
	}

	# The READER, at its own address and with its own verb: `/em/read/` is where
	# somebody who was handed a link READS a study, and `/em/studio/` is where
	# somebody WORKS on one. Two doors, two verbs — the rule the front door's
	# three cards already follow.
	#
	# In development both pages come out of ONE dev server, which can only have
	# one base, so this rewrites onto the reader's page under that base. In
	# production they are two files under one root and the block below is
	# simpler — see `heriverse-ansible/role/templates/Caddyfile.j2`.
	handle /em/read/* {
		# the PATH only: `rewrite` leaves the query string alone, which is the
		# whole point here — `?study=` and `?emjson=` have to survive
		rewrite * /em/studio/reader.html
		reverse_proxy host.docker.internal:5173
	}

	# StratiGraph · the room (graph + assets + the WebSocket the editors join).
	# `handle_path` strips `/em`, because StratiGraph Server serves `/v1/...` at its
	# root. The `/em` PATH stays: it is a public address, not the repo's name.
	handle_path /em/* {
		reverse_proxy stratigraph-server:8000
	}

	# StratiGraph · the FIELD ASSISTANT — and the reason it is here at all is a
	# browser rule, not a preference: camera, microphone and GPS are offered
	# only to a secure context. On a laptop `http://localhost:8020` counted as
	# one; on a PHONE it does not, so the assistant ran without the three things
	# it exists for. Behind this door it is https on the shared origin, and they
	# come back.
	#
	# `handle_path` — which STRIPS — and that is the opposite of the two routes
	# below it. The distinction the rest of this file keeps warning about, so
	# here is which side this one is on and why:
	#
	#   · the Catalog serves `/catalog/...` at its own root, and IIIF carries its
	#     API version in the path. For them the prefix IS part of the endpoint,
	#     so stripping it would take the route with it → `handle`.
	#   · the assistant serves `/`, `/v1/...`, `/sw.js` and `/brand/...` at its
	#     root, exactly like the room server. It knows nothing about `/chat`, so
	#     the prefix has to come off → `handle_path`.
	#
	# The PAGE is what makes this safe: it derives its API base and its service
	# worker from its own document URL (`web/index.html`, `web/sw.js`), so the
	# same file works served here at `/chat/` and at `:8020/` for the `--reload`
	# loop. A worker registered at the origin's root would claim scope `/` and
	# intercept the fetches of every service above.
	handle_path /chat/* {
		reverse_proxy stratigraph-chatbot:8000
	}

	# StratiGraph · the CATALOG (studies as published, beside the rooms above).
	# `handle` and NOT `handle_path`: StratiGraph Catalog serves `/catalog/...` at its
	# root, so the prefix is part of the endpoint and stripping it would take
	# the route with it — the same distinction as `/iiif` below, and the same
	# trap.
	handle /catalog/* {
		reverse_proxy stratigraph-catalog:8000
	}

	# The IIIF Image API. `handle` and NOT `handle_path`: the API version lives
	# in the path (`/iiif/3/...`), so stripping the prefix would take the
	# endpoint with it.
	#
	# EMBARGO · every image request is authorised by StratiGraph Server FIRST.
	#
	# Cantaloupe reads the bucket by sha256 and the server is not in the path of
	# a pixel: taking the canvas out of a manifest withholds the digest, but
	# whoever already has the hex reaches the image directly. So the proxy asks
	# — `forward_auth` to `/v1/iiif-authz`, which reads the graph, resolves the
	# room's roles and applies the SAME rule the asset route applies (editor and
	# above may see an embargoed image; nobody else may).
	#
	# `X-Forwarded-Uri` is what carries the identifier: the sub-request goes to
	# a fixed path, so the original one has to travel in a header. `copy_headers
	# X-EM-Authz` brings the decision back for anybody debugging it.
	#
	# Chosen over a Cantaloupe DELEGATE script (Ruby, calling back here): a
	# delegate puts a second implementation of the rule in a second language,
	# inside a component whose job is not authorisation, and hides it from the
	# file people actually read when they ask who can reach what.
	handle /iiif/* {
		forward_auth stratigraph-server:8000 {
			uri /v1/iiif-authz
			copy_headers X-EM-Authz
		}
		reverse_proxy cantaloupe:8182
	}

	# The object store, for clients that fetch an asset by its digest.
	handle_path /assets/* {
		reverse_proxy minio:9000
	}

	# Identity, on the same origin as everything else.
	handle /auth/* {
		reverse_proxy keycloak:8080
	}

	# TWO blocks, and the split is a bug fix rather than tidying.
	#
	# There used to be one `handle` answering **200** with this banner to
	# ANYTHING unrouted. Measured on 2026-08-30: a client that posted to the
	# wrong base got `200 OK · text/plain` instead of a 404, died on
	# `res.json()`, and its catch reported «node unreachable» — while the node
	# was up. A field note dictated in a trench would have sat in an offline
	# queue for ever, with a message blaming the network. That is not the
	# chatbot's bug: it is every client we will ever connect.
	#
	# `handle /` matches the root PATH EXACTLY — a matcher without a trailing
	# `*` is not a prefix (measured below, not assumed: `/anything` falls
	# through to the 404). So the banner keeps helping a person who lands here,
	# and a program that missed the route is told it missed.
	# …and what it points at is now the NODE'S FRONT DOOR: the room browser,
	# grown a head — who you are, what this node runs, and the three scopes.
	#
	# A REDIRECT and not a proxy, and that is the whole care of these four lines.
	# The page is served at `/em/rooms/` and asks for its neighbours relatively
	# (`../admin/console.css`, `../brand/…`): proxied onto `/` those would resolve
	# to `/admin/…` and `/brand/…`, which are not routes on this origin — so the
	# page would arrive unstyled, and the 404 below would be blamed.
	#
	# It also keeps the page from PRESUMING it owns the root. In production it
	# does not: `role/templates/Caddyfile.j2` opens with `redir / /a/heriverse`,
	# because on a node that also carries Heriverse the root is Heriverse's.
	# Where this page sits is a routing decision per deployment, never a property
	# of the page — which is why the page derives its API base from its own URL.
	handle / {
		# `redir * <to> <code>` — the `*` is NOT decoration. `redir` reads a
		# leading `/` as a PATH MATCHER, so `redir /em/rooms/ 302` parses as
		# "match /em/rooms/, redirect to 302" and answers 200 with an empty body:
		# measured, and it looks exactly like a working root until you read the
		# content-length. Inside `handle /` the path is already decided, so the
		# matcher is `*`.
		redir * /em/rooms/ 302
	}

	# WHAT AN UPSTREAM THAT IS NOT THERE SAYS. One block, because `handle_errors`
	# is a site-level directive: Caddy refuses it inside a `handle` («not an
	# ordered HTTP handler»), which is a good refusal — an error page is a
	# property of the site, not of one route.
	#
	# The EMStudio routes get their own sentence because their upstream is a dev
	# server on somebody's laptop, i.e. the one upstream here whose absence is
	# ORDINARY. Everything else keeps the status and says the path, which is what
	# a program needs and what a person can paste into a bug report.
	handle_errors {
		@emstudio path /em/studio/* /em/read/*
		handle @emstudio {
			respond "{err.status_code} — EMStudio's dev server is not answering on the host (:5173).

Start it:   cd EMStudio/frontend && npm run dev
It serves /em/studio/ (the editor) and /em/studio/reader.html (the reader),
and this origin proxies straight through to it.

In production these two routes are served from the built files instead
(heriverse-ansible role/templates/Caddyfile.j2), so this message belongs to
development only." {err.status_code}
		}
		handle {
			respond "{err.status_code} on {uri} — this node's proxy could not reach the service behind that route." {err.status_code}
		}
	}

	handle {
		# Plain text on purpose: a JSON body here would be a guess about who is
		# asking, and the point is that this route belongs to nobody.
		#
		# The list is HERE rather than behind "see /", because / is now a door and
		# not an index — and a 404 that sends a program to a redirect has told it
		# nothing.
		respond "no such route on this node.

WHERE YOU START AGAIN:  /em/rooms/   — the front door (sign in and it also draws
                                       the node map: every face, its state, and
                                       the curl to ask it the same question)

others: /em/v1/health · /em/studio/ · /catalog/health · /chat/ · /iiif/3/<sha256>/info.json" 404
	}
}
